f7a-d28c89ce7488","name":"Shiva temple, Kera","deity":"Shiva","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Gujarat","district":"Kachchh","town":"Kundanpar","latitude":23.08814,"longitude":69.59368,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Rukmawati River","river_distance_km":13.4,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:04:44.755986+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Also called the Lakheshwara temple of Kerakot, this Shiva temple stands in Kera village near Bhuj in Kutch. It was built under the Chaulukya (Solanki) dynasty in the later 10th century. The 1819 and 2001 earthquakes badly damaged it, yet its spire, sanctum and sculptures survive in fair condition.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Bhuj Rudra Mata Air Force Base","nearest_airport_distance_km":22,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Sukhpur railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":13.5,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Shiva temple, Kera), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":10},{"id":"d8176fae-d977-4e57-8b34-8d7c60f68e1d","name":"Kailasanathar Temple, Thingalur","deity":"Kailasanathar (Shiva) / Periyanayaki","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":["Navagraha Sthalam"],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Thanjavur","town":"Thingalur","latitude":10.88667,"longitude":79.12556,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Cauvery","river_distance_km":2,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-05T11:47:03.785954+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:00.624755+00:00","sthala_purana":"The Navagraha Sthalam for Chandra (the Moon). Tradition holds Chandra married the 28 daughters of Daksha but favoured only the youngest, provoking the others to complain to their father, who cursed Chandra to lose his radiance; worship of Kailasanathar at this thousand-plus-year-old temple is said to have restored it. During the Tamil months of Purattasi and Panguni, moonlight is said to fall directly on the presiding deity. Believed founded by the Pallava king Rajasimha in the 7th century, with later Chola and Nayaka-era renovations.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"கைலாசநாதர் கோயில், திங்களூர்","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Thanjavur Air Force Station","nearest_airport_distance_km":18.3,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Pasupatikovil railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":7.2,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Puranic tradition (Daksha/Chandra legend); local/regional tradition","architecture_style":"Dravidian (Pallava origins, Chola and Nayaka renovations)","construction_century":7},{"id":"d8535896-469b-46ea-a4ce-8b6970d9a94f","name":"Billeswar Devalaya","deity":"Shiva as Bilbeswar","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Assam","district":"Nalbari","town":"Pipalibari","latitude":26.38183,"longitude":91.35261,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Tihu Nadi River","river_distance_km":6.3,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:38:36.086063+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Bilbeswar Devalaya at Belsor in Nalbari district, Assam, is an ancient Shiva temple believed to be about 500 years old. A buffalo sacrifice during Durga Puja is described as a long-standing ritual here.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Lokpriya Gopinath Bordoloi International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":38,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Kaithalkuchi railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":7.7,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Billeswar Devalaya), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"d88600ab-964e-4010-8ff8-d9c935e0af13","name":"Rajarani Temple, Bhubaneswar","deity":"None today — originally dedicated to Shiva as Indreshvara; no longer an active place of worship","sampradaya":null,"significance":["ASI monument, not an active temple"],"country":"India","state":"Odisha","district":"Khurda","town":"Bhubaneswar","latitude":20.24344,"longitude":85.84352,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Dayaa River","river_distance_km":3.5,"best_season_notes":"The Rajarani Music Festival (Hindustani, Carnatic and Odissi performances), held annually 18–20 January, is a cultural event here rather than a religious one — consistent with the temple's non-worship status.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-22T08:41:30.569832+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Originally known as Indreshvara and dedicated to Shiva, the sanctum today holds no deity image, and the site is maintained as a ticketed ASI monument rather than a place of worship. Its name comes from the reddish-gold (\"raja\") and yellow sandstone it's built from, not from any royal couple. Dated to the mid-11th century CE on stylistic grounds, it's known for a tall clustered spire and richly carved feminine and erotic figures — earning it the local nickname \"the love temple\" — and some scholars see its style as influential on temples later built around Khajuraho, though the direction and degree of that influence is debated. Its jagamohana (porch) had collapsed at some point and was restored in 1903.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Biju Patnaik International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":3.3,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Bhubaneswar railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":2.6,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Art-historical scholarship; Wikipedia","architecture_style":"Kalinga pancharatha style; reddish-gold and yellow sandstone, roughly 18m clustered shikhara spire","construction_century":11},{"id":"d89f15a7-30af-4199-9133-3f35da47573d","name":"Kondagattu Anjaneya Swamy Temple","deity":"Anjaneya (Hanuman)","sampradaya":null,"significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Telangana","district":"Jagtial","town":"Muthyumpeta (Mallial mandal)","latitude":18.4045,"longitude":78.5521,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Maneru River","