holds Shiva's matted locks (jata) surfaced after the Pandavas' pursuit of him following the Mahabharata war — he is worshipped here as Jatadhar, \"bearer of the matted hair.\" The temple's priests are traditionally Dasnami Gossains, disciples of Adi Shankaracharya.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"कल्पेश्वर मंदिर","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Dehradun Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":126.5,"nearest_railway_station_name":null,"nearest_railway_distance_km":null,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"e3b50e46-6f7d-4b09-92fb-0878f3b70194","name":"Marundheeswarar Temple, Tiruvanmiyur","deity":"Marundheeswarar (Shiva) / Tripurasundari","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Chennai","town":"Tiruvanmiyur","latitude":12.9822,"longitude":80.2593,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Bay of Bengal","river_distance_km":1,"best_season_notes":"Nov–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-08T06:51:40.218447+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:00.624755+00:00","sthala_purana":"Reported to be around 1,300 years old, this Shiva temple's name — \"the lord of medicine\" — comes from a tradition that the sage Valmiki was cured of an ailment here; it is glorified in the Tevaram hymns of the Nayanmar saints as one of the Padal Petra Sthalams.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Chennai International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":10,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Tiruvanmiyur railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1.1,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Contributor-submitted local survey; local/regional tradition; Tevaram","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"e3db475f-9954-4e6b-bb5e-a8bad4b1d831","name":"Neelakantheswara Temple, Nizamabad","deity":"Shiva and Parvati (Neelakanta)","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Telangana","district":"Nizamabad","town":"Nizamabad North mandal","latitude":18.6854,"longitude":78.1145,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Phulang Vagu River","river_distance_km":0.5,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:04:47.875457+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A Shiva temple in Nizamabad, Telangana, where the presiding deities are Shiva, known as Neelakanta, and Parvati. According to the Puranas, Brahma worshipped Shiva's idol here. It is regarded as ancient, and devotees gather in large numbers at Shivaratri.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Shri Guru Gobind Singh Ji Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":99.6,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Nizamabad Junction railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1.4,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Neelakantheswara Temple, Nizamabad), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"e3df5592-6f08-4f7a-a920-86347e6f6832","name":"Chausath Yogini Temple, Bhedaghat","deity":"The Yoginis — 81 images in nine groups of nine, despite the traditional \"64\" in the name — with a central Gauri-Shankar (Shiva-Parvati) shrine, originally a Bhairava/dancing-Shiva shrine","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":["Tantric shrine","Largest known circular yogini temple in India"],"country":"India","state":"Madhya Pradesh","district":"Jabalpur","town":"Bhedaghat","latitude":23.1297,"longitude":79.8013,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Narmada","river_distance_km":0.5,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-22T09:07:42.20596+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-22T09:07:42.20596+00:00","sthala_purana":"Built in the early 11th century by the Kalachuri king Yuvaraja II (Yuvarajdev I, reigned roughly 975–1025 CE), with the central shrine remodelled in 1155 CE into its present Gauri-Shankar form. The town was historically known as Bhairavaghat. This history is well documented by scholars including Shaman Hatley, who read the unusual count of 81 (rather than the canonical 64) yoginis as a mark of royal patronage.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":"Approximately 7am–8:30pm per travel sources; Madhya Pradesh Tourism does not publish exact hours.","accessibility_notes":"Hilltop site above the Narmada marble rocks at Bhedaghat; some climbing is required to reach it.","nearest_airport_name":"Jabalpur (Dumna) Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":34.4,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Jabalpur Junction","nearest_railway_distance_km":20.9,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Kalachuri dynasty records; art-historical scholarship (Shaman Hatley and others)","architecture_style":"Circular yogini temple roughly 125ft in diameter — the largest known circular yogini temple in India — with a covered walkway of 81 cells and three entrance niches","construction_century":11},{"id":"e3e114a5-75d7-41eb-9d45-a03193182adf","name":"Jagannatha Perumal Temple, Thirumazhisai","deity":"Jagannathan (Vishnu)","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Tiruvallur","town":"Thirumazhisai","latitude":13.0797,"longitude":80.0611,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Cooum River","river_distance_km":2.4,"best_season_notes":"Nov–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-08T06:51:40.218447+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Reported to be about 1,000 years old, one of the 108 Divya Desams, and the birthplace of the Alvar poet-saint Thirumazhisai Alvar.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Chennai International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":15,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Hindu College railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":4.5,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Contributor-submitted local survey; Nalayira Divya Prabandham","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"e4182450-52a5-4502-999e-5e92c8f54167","name":"Jageshwar Temple Complex","deity":"Shiva, worshipped across a cluster of 125+ shrines","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Uttarakhand","district":"Almora","town":"Jageshwar","latitude":29.633,"longitude":79.85,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Jaigan Nadi River","river_distance_km":6.5,"best_season_notes":"A high-altitude Himalayan site (about 1,870 m); best visited outside the harsh Himalayan winter.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-30T13:17:24.643882+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"The complex is a grouping of more than 125 stone temples, mostly dedicated to Shiva, built up over roughly the 7th to 14th centuries CE — among the largest single clusters of stone temples anywhere. Some sources, including Uttarakhand's own tourism department, describe it as one of the twelve Jyotirlingas; Wikipedia treats this more cautiously, calling it a major Kumaon Shiva pilgrimage site without asserting canonical Jyotirlinga status. The two claims are presented here as a live disagreement, not resolved.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Uttarakhand","nearest_airport_distance_km":null,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Kathgodam railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":null,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Jageshwar); Uttarakhand government temple site (jageshwar-jyotirlinga.uk.gov.in)","architecture_style":"Nagara (North Indian) style with some hill-temple influence; ASI-protected, with an on-site ASI museum","construction_century":null},{"id":"e43e3dc1-30b8-4547-b6bc-b1a6d0c76d14","name":"Virupakshiswarar Temple, Mylapore","deity":"Virupaksheeswarar (Shiva) / Visalakshi","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significan